\title[Faithful-model slackness]{Faithful-Model Slackness in the Exponent-One-Fifth Factoring Cost Model:\\ Two Certificates, Deletion Penalties, and a Sharp Coupling Threshold}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Harvey's deterministic integer factorisation algorithm runs in time $N^{1/5}$ up to logarithmic factors. Its parameter balance is often analysed through a three-leg cost $\max\{r,\, m,\, N^{1/2}/(r^{1/2}m)\}$, made up of a pair floor $r$, a baby-step budget $m$ and a giant-step interior. This relaxation is minimised exactly at $r=m=N^{1/5}$. It leaves out a leg the algorithm actually pays for: one step fixes an auxiliary length $M=\lceil (N/r)^{1/2}\rceil$, which costs $(N/r)^{1/4}$. We study the four-leg \emph{faithful} model
\[
T(r,m)=\max\Big\{(N/r)^{1/4},\ \frac{N^{1/2}}{r^{1/2}m},\ r,\ m\Big\}.
\]
The two legs $(N/r)^{1/4}$ and $r$ alone already force $N^{1/5}$, so the interior and baby-step legs are \emph{slack}. Both models have the same unique minimiser. The value $1/5$ has two certificates that share only the pair floor, and any exponent below $1/5$ requires breaking both. Deleting the baby-step floor costs $N^{2/15}$ and deleting the pair floor costs $N^{1/20}$. A fourth search floor of weight $w$ gives $1/(5+2w)$ in the relaxation but nothing in the faithful model. For $\max\{(N/r)^\mu, N^\gamma/(r^\alpha m), r, m\}$ the optimal exponent is exactly $\max(\mu/(1+\mu),\gamma/(2+\alpha))$. If the $M$-leg is coupled to the baby steps as $(N/r)^{1/4}/m^\theta$, the exponent $1/6$ is reachable with a weight-$\tfrac12$ fourth floor if and only if $\theta\ge 1/4$. Finally, any multi-level baby-step/giant-step structure over a pool $S$ costs at least $2\sqrt S$, strictly more when two levels are active, and $\sqrt k$ times more for the uniform $k$-way split. All results hold inside an assumed cost shape. None is a lower bound for factoring.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background}
Deterministic integer factorisation has a long sequence of exponents. Trial division factors $N$ in $O(N^{1/2})$ operations. Lehman's method achieves $O(N^{1/3})$~\cite{lehman}. The Pollard--Strassen method achieves $N^{1/4}$ up to logarithms~\cite{pollard,strassen}. Harvey~\cite{harvey} gave a deterministic algorithm with complexity $N^{1/5}$ up to logarithmic factors. It combines a Lehman-style search over about $r$ candidate pairs with a baby-step/giant-step search in the spirit of Shanks~\cite{shanks}, using fast polynomial multipoint evaluation.

Asymptotic analyses of such algorithms usually reduce the running time to the \emph{maximum of a few legs}, each a monomial in $N$ and the parameters. After taking logarithms base $N$, choosing the parameters becomes a small linear program.

\subsection{The question and the missing leg}
A widely used simplified model has three legs,
\[
T_{\mathrm{rel}}(r,m)=\max\Big\{r,\ m,\ \frac{N^{1/2}}{r^{1/2}m}\Big\},
\]
with optimum exactly $N^{1/5}$. It suggests three co-binding legs, so that cheapening the baby steps, restructuring the interior, or adding a search parameter that shrinks the interior should each lower the exponent.

One step of the algorithm fixes $M=\lceil (N/r)^{1/2}\rceil$, and computing with $M$ terms costs $(N/r)^{1/4}$ up to logarithms. We adopt this as the modelling hypothesis. The faithful cost is
\[
T(r,m)=\max\Big\{(N/r)^{1/4},\ \frac{N^{1/2}}{r^{1/2}m},\ r,\ m\Big\}.
\]
Adding this leg does not change the optimum, but it changes which legs certify the optimum.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $N>1$ and all costs are taken up to polylogarithmic factors.

\begin{definition}[Exponent coordinates]
Write $r=N^a$ and $m=N^b$. A monomial cost $N^{e(a,b)}$ with $e$ affine is identified with its exponent $e$. The exponent of a maximum of monomials is the maximum of the exponents.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Legs]
The four legs of the faithful model are:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
leg & cost & exponent\\
\midrule
$M$-leg & $(N/r)^{1/4}$ & $(1-a)/4$\\
interior & $N^{1/2}/(r^{1/2}m)$ & $\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b$\\
pair floor & $r$ & $a$\\
baby-step floor & $m$ & $b$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Relaxed and faithful exponents]
\[
\Erel(a,b)=\max\Big\{a,\ b,\ \tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b\Big\},\qquad
E(a,b)=\max\Big\{\tfrac{1-a}{4},\ \Erel(a,b)\Big\},
\]
and the pair-leg core is $P(a)=\max\{(1-a)/4,\ a\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Certificate, slack]
Let $\mathcal L$ be a finite set of affine legs with optimum $v^\ast=\inf\max_{\ell\in\mathcal L}\ell$. A subset $\mathcal C\subseteq\mathcal L$ is a \emph{certificate} for $v$ if $\max_{\ell\in\mathcal C}\ell\ge v$ everywhere. A set of legs is \emph{removable} (or \emph{slack}) if its complement is a certificate for $v^\ast$. A slack leg may still be tight at the optimum. Slackness means the bound does not depend on it.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fourth floor, coupling]
A fourth floor of weight $w\ge0$ is a new parameter $N^c$ costing $N^c$ and dividing the interior by $N^{wc}$:
\[
E^{(4)}_{\mathrm{rel}}(a,b,c;w)=\max\Big\{a,\ b,\ c,\ \tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b-wc\Big\},\quad
E^{(4)}=\max\Big\{\tfrac{1-a}{4},\ E^{(4)}_{\mathrm{rel}}\Big\}.
\]
A coupling of strength $\theta$ replaces the $M$-leg exponent by $\tfrac{1-a}{4}-\theta b$, that is, the $M$-leg cost by $(N/r)^{1/4}/m^{\theta}$. We write $E^{(4)}_\theta$ for the resulting cost.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Generalised faithful exponent]
For $\mu>0$, $\gamma\in\mathbb R$, $\alpha\ge0$:
$G_{\mu,\gamma,\alpha}(a,b)=\max\{\mu(1-a),\ \gamma-\alpha a-b,\ a,\ b\}$, the exponent of $\max\{(N/r)^\mu, N^\gamma/(r^\alpha m), r, m\}$. Harvey's model is $(\tfrac14,\tfrac12,\tfrac12)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Basic bounds and the unique minimiser}

\begin{theorem}[Relaxed lower bound]\label{thm:relax}
$\Erel(a,b)\ge 1/5$ for all real $a,b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $t=\Erel(a,b)$. Adding the interior inequality, half the pair inequality and the baby-step inequality gives
$\tfrac12=(\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b)+\tfrac12a+b\le t+\tfrac12t+t=\tfrac52t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$E(a,b)\ge\Erel(a,b)\ge1/5$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Attainment]
$E(\tfrac15,\tfrac15)=\Erel(\tfrac15,\tfrac15)=\tfrac15$ and all four legs equal $\tfrac15$ there. In real form, $(N/N^{1/5})^{1/4}=N^{1/5}$ and $N^{1/2}/((N^{1/5})^{1/2}N^{1/5})=N^{1/5}$ for all $N>0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Unique minimiser]\label{thm:unique}
If $E(a,b)\le1/5$ then $a=b=1/5$. The same holds for $\Erel$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Faithful case: $(1-a)/4\le1/5$ gives $a\ge1/5$, so $a=1/5$. Then $\tfrac12-\tfrac1{10}-b\le\tfrac15$ gives $b\ge\tfrac15$, so $b=\tfrac15$. Relaxed case: $a,b\le\tfrac15$ and $\tfrac a2+b\ge\tfrac3{10}$, while $\tfrac a2+b\le\tfrac3{10}$, so equality holds throughout.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The $M$-leg is a geometric mean]
For every $(a,b)$, $\frac{1-a}{4}=\frac12\big[(\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b)+b\big]$, that is, $(N/r)^{1/4}=\sqrt{\frac{N^{1/2}}{r^{1/2}m}\cdot m}$. So the $M$-leg never strictly exceeds both the interior and the baby-step leg, and $E(a,b)=\Erel(a,b)$ identically. In the unmodified model the $M$-leg only ties the maximum, along the segment $b=(1-a)/4$, $0\le a\le\tfrac15$. The identity breaks as soon as the interior or the baby steps are cheapened without cheapening the $M$-stage, and then the $M$-leg binds.
\end{remark}

\section{Faithful-model slackness}

\begin{theorem}[Pair-leg core]\label{thm:pair}
$P(a)\ge1/5$ for all real $a$, with equality iff $a=1/5$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $a\ge1/5$ the pair term suffices. Otherwise $(1-a)/4>1/5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Slackness, exponent form]\label{thm:slack}
For all real $a,f,g$: $\max\{\tfrac{1-a}{4},\ f,\ a,\ g\}\ge\tfrac15$. So the interior and the baby-step floor are removable: replacing them by arbitrary values, or deleting them, does not lower the optimum.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Slackness, real-power form]\label{thm:slackreal}
For all real $N>0$, $r>0$ and arbitrary real $f,g$:
\[
\max\Big\{(N/r)^{1/4},\ f,\ r,\ g\Big\}\ \ge\ N^{1/5}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Otherwise $(N/r)^{1/4}<N^{1/5}$ and $r<N^{1/5}$. The fourth power of the first gives $N<N^{4/5}r$. Multiplying the second by $N^{4/5}$ gives $N^{4/5}r<N$. Together these give $N<N$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

At the operating point all four legs are tight, but the bound $N^{1/5}$ needs only the $M$-leg and the pair floor. No improvement to the interior or baby-step leg, of any size, can move the exponent while those two keep their costs. The three-leg model is a valid lower bound, but it is a \emph{relaxation}: its binding legs are not the only ones the algorithm pays for.

\section{Two certificates; improvement must break both}

By Theorems~\ref{thm:relax} and~\ref{thm:slack} there are two minimal certificates for $1/5$:
\emph{Certificate A} $=\{M\text{-leg},\ \text{pair}\}$ and \emph{Certificate B} $=\{\text{interior},\ \text{pair},\ \text{baby}\}$. They share only the pair floor. Minimality holds because removing any one leg leaves a set of affine functions whose maximum is unbounded below.

\begin{theorem}[Improvement must break both certificates]\label{thm:both}
Let $M',f,g$ be arbitrary reals (a modified $M$-leg, interior and baby-step leg) and keep the pair floor at exponent $a$. If $\max\{M',f,a,g\}<\tfrac15$, then
\begin{enumerate}
\item $M'<\tfrac{1-a}{4}$, and
\item for every $b\in\mathbb R$: $f<\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b$ or $g<b$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
From the hypothesis, $a<\tfrac15$, so $\tfrac{1-a}{4}>\tfrac15>M'$. If for some $b$ both $f\ge\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b$ and $g\ge b$, then $\max\{f,a,g\}\ge\Erel(a,b)\ge\tfrac15$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Improving only the $M$-leg, or only the \{interior, baby-step\} family, never lowers the exponent below $1/5$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Floor deletions and their penalties}

\begin{proposition}[Baby-step floor deletion]
$E(a,0)\ge\tfrac13$ for all $a$, and $E(\tfrac13,0)=\tfrac13$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If $a\ge\tfrac13$ the pair leg suffices. Otherwise the interior $\tfrac12-\tfrac a2>\tfrac13$. At $a=\tfrac13$ the legs are $\tfrac16,\tfrac13,0,\tfrac13$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Pair floor deletion]
$E(0,b)\ge\tfrac14$ for all $b$, and $E(0,\tfrac14)=\tfrac14$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
With $a=0$ the $M$-leg equals $\tfrac14$. At $b=\tfrac14$ the other legs are $\tfrac14,0,\tfrac14$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Attribution]
The deletion penalties are $\tfrac13-\tfrac15=\tfrac2{15}$ for the baby-step floor and $\tfrac14-\tfrac15=\tfrac1{20}$ for the pair floor. A slowdown of $N^{1/20}$ therefore comes from deleting the pair floor, which returns the algorithm to the Pollard--Strassen exponent. It does not come from deleting the baby-step floor.
\end{corollary}

\section{The fourth-floor lever}

\begin{theorem}[Fourth floor in the relaxation]\label{thm:fourrel}
For $w\ge0$, $\min E^{(4)}_{\mathrm{rel}}(a,b,c;w)=\frac{1}{5+2w}$, attained at $a=b=c=\frac1{5+2w}$. In particular $w=\tfrac12$ gives $\tfrac16$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\tfrac12=(\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b-wc)+\tfrac a2+b+wc\le t(\tfrac52+w)$ using $a,b,c\le t$ and $w\ge0$. At $a=b=c=t=1/(5+2w)$ the interior is $\tfrac12-t(\tfrac32+w)=t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The lever is blocked in the faithful model]\label{thm:blocked}
For every $w$ and all $a,b,c$: $E^{(4)}(a,b,c;w)\ge\tfrac15$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$E^{(4)}\ge P(a)\ge\tfrac15$. The new floor does not enter the $M$-leg.
\end{proof}

\section{The independent certificate law}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:mu}
For $\mu>0$: $\max\{\mu(1-a),a\}\ge\frac{\mu}{1+\mu}$ for all $a$, with equality at $a=\frac{\mu}{1+\mu}$. Harvey's $\mu=\tfrac14$ gives $\tfrac15$, and reaching $\tfrac16$ with the $\{M,\text{pair}\}$ core requires $\mu\le\tfrac15$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
This uses the identity $\mu(1-\frac{\mu}{1+\mu})=\frac{\mu}{1+\mu}$ and monotonicity, since $\mu>0$. Also $\frac\mu{1+\mu}\le\frac16$ iff $\mu\le\frac15$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:triple}
For $\alpha\ge0$: $\max\{\gamma-\alpha a-b,\ a,\ b\}\ge\frac{\gamma}{2+\alpha}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\gamma=(\gamma-\alpha a-b)+\alpha a+b\le(2+\alpha)t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Independent certificate law]\label{thm:law}
For $\mu>0$, $\alpha\ge0$, $\gamma\in\mathbb R$:
\[
\min_{a,b}G_{\mu,\gamma,\alpha}(a,b)=\max\Big(\frac{\mu}{1+\mu},\ \frac{\gamma}{2+\alpha}\Big),
\]
attained at $a=b=t^\ast$, where $t^\ast$ is the right-hand side.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The lower bound follows from Proposition~\ref{prop:mu} and Lemma~\ref{lem:triple}. At $a=b=t^\ast$: $\mu\le t^\ast(1+\mu)$ gives $\mu(1-t^\ast)\le t^\ast$, and $\gamma\le t^\ast(2+\alpha)$ gives $\gamma-\alpha t^\ast-t^\ast\le t^\ast$.
\end{proof}

The two certificates do not interact. For Harvey's parameters $\frac{1/4}{5/4}=\frac15=\frac{1/2}{5/2}$, which is why all four legs tie.

\section{Coupling the $M$-leg: a sharp threshold}

\begin{theorem}[Coupling alone is useless]
For all $\theta,a,b$: $\max\{\tfrac{1-a}{4}-\theta b,\ \Erel(a,b)\}\ge\tfrac15$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Certificate B is untouched (Theorem~\ref{thm:relax}).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Coupled threshold]\label{thm:coupled}
With a fourth floor of weight $\tfrac12$: $E^{(4)}_\theta(a,b,c;\tfrac12)\ge\tfrac16$ always, and there exist $a,b,c$ with $E^{(4)}_\theta(a,b,c;\tfrac12)\le\tfrac16$ if and only if $\theta\ge\tfrac14$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The lower bound is Theorem~\ref{thm:fourrel}. If the cost is at most $\tfrac16$, then $a,b,c\le\tfrac16$ and $\tfrac a2+b+\tfrac c2\ge\tfrac13$, which forces $a=b=c=\tfrac16$. Then $\tfrac5{24}-\tfrac\theta6\le\tfrac16$ iff $\theta\ge\tfrac14$. Conversely, the point $a=b=c=\tfrac16$ works when $\theta\ge\tfrac14$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
For intermediate couplings exact linear programming gives $2/11$ at $\theta=1/8$ and $5/29$ at $\theta=1/5$. The guess ``below $1/5$ for every $\theta>0$'' is false without a new floor.
\end{remark}

\section{The partition lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Partition lemma]\label{thm:partition}
Let $S_i\ge0$, $S=\sum_iS_i$, and $L_i,T_i\ge0$ with $L_iT_i\ge S_i$. Then $\sum_iL_i+\sum_iT_i\ge2\sqrt S$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By induction, $4S\le(\sum_i(L_i+T_i))^2$. With $U=\sum_{i<k}(L_i+T_i)\ge0$,
\[
(L_k+T_k+U)^2\ge(L_k+T_k)^2+U^2\ge4L_kT_k+U^2\ge4S_k+4\textstyle\sum_{i<k}S_i.
\]
Taking square roots gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict partition lemma]
If two distinct levels have $S_i,S_j>0$, the inequality is strict. So equality forces a single level to carry the whole pool.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$S_i>0$ forces $L_i+T_i>0$, and similarly for $j$. The cross term $2(L_i+T_i)(L_j+T_j)>0$ then makes the squared inequality strict.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Uniform split penalty]
For $k\ge1$, the uniform split $S_i=S/k$, $L_i=T_i=\sqrt{S/k}$ costs $k\cdot2\sqrt{S/k}=\sqrt k\cdot2\sqrt S$.
\end{proposition}

So variable-step and multi-level baby-step/giant-step schemes are ruled out by a theorem. In the interior, the product $m\cdot N^{1/2}/(r^{1/2}m)=N^{1/2}r^{-1/2}$ does not depend on $m$, so by AM--GM no re-split beats the balanced one.

\section{Computational procedures}

\subsection*{Exact min--max exponent by vertex enumeration}
To minimise $t$ subject to $t\ge\ell_j(x)$ for affine legs $\ell_j$ and box constraints on $x\in\mathbb R^n$: list all constraints, solve every $(n+1)\times(n+1)$ subsystem of tight constraints exactly over $\mathbb Q$, keep the feasible solutions, and return the smallest $t$. With $K$ constraints this takes $\binom{K}{n+1}$ exact solves, at most $330$ for the models here.

\subsection*{Certificate extraction}
Enumerate subsets of legs in increasing size and compute each subset's optimum. A subset is recorded as a minimal certificate if it reproduces the full optimum and contains no earlier certificate. For the faithful model this returns exactly Certificates A and B.

\section{Discussion}

All statements are theorems about an \emph{assumed} cost shape, up to logarithms. $N^{1/5}$ is an attained upper bound, not a floor. The results rule out cheapening the baby-step table, which is anyway already built by a product tree and evaluated with a Bluestein-type chirp transform~\cite{bluestein}. They also rule out restructuring the interior, adding a fourth floor alone, and coupling alone. An improvement to exponent $E$ must push both $\mu/(1+\mu)$ and the interior certificate below $E$. Within the coupled family, $1/6$ requires exactly a weight-$\tfrac12$ new floor and coupling $\theta\ge\tfrac14$.

The three-leg relaxation reproduces the correct optimum and the correct unique minimiser, yet its account of which legs bind is wrong for the algorithm. Matching the optimum of a cost model does not certify its binding structure.

\emph{An empirical remark, not used.} Experiments suggest that the exponent bit-lengths in the pair family satisfy $c_{2a,2b}=2c_{a,b}+\delta$ with $\delta\in\{0,1\}$. If true, the pair family needs $O(r)$ rather than $O(r\log r)$ modular exponentiations. A logarithmic saving does not change the exponent of $N$.

\section{Future directions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{A coupled $M$-leg.} Arrange the $M$-stage to reuse the baby steps so that its cost is $(N/r)^{1/4}/m^\theta$ with $\theta\ge\tfrac14$. Together with a weight-$\tfrac12$ floor this gives $1/6$ in the model.
\item \emph{Real-power certificate law.} $\inf_{r,m>0}\max\{(N/r)^\mu,N^\gamma/(r^\alpha m),r,m\}=N^{\max(\mu/(1+\mu),\gamma/(2+\alpha))}$.
\item \emph{Weighted partition lemma.} With level costs $c_i(L_i+T_i)$, $c_i>0$: the total is at least $2(\min_ic_i)\sqrt S$.
\item \emph{Deriving the cost shape} $(\mu,\gamma,\alpha)=(\tfrac14,\tfrac12,\tfrac12)$ and admissible $(\theta,w)$ from an explicit algorithm.
\end{enumerate}

